Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["", "SlideShowNavigationBar",
 CellTags->"SlideShowHeader"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Slide 3", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.468511948489377*^9, 3.468511952700135*^9}},
 FontSize->18],

Cell["\<\
Problem avoided if particles were endowed with internal \"instruction sets,\" \
written onto \"hidden variables.\" Probabilistic aspects of quantum mechanics \
would then be a reflection of our ignorance of the state of the hidden \
variables\[Ellipsis]and it was that kind of ignorance that in EPR's view \
rendered quantum mechanics \"incomplete.\"

Bohr immediatey wrote a strong rebuttal (which Bell later held to be \"almost \
unintelligible\"), and anyway it was widely believed that John von Neumann \
(1932) had shown hidden variable theories to be impossible (by a line of \
argument that Bell later showed to contain a fatal flaw).
  
So Pauli, Born and nearly everybody else was happy to dismiss the whole \
issue.  
  
Schr\[ODoubleDot]dinger, however, considered (1935) that EPR had identified \
the most profoundly puzzling/distinctive feature of quantum theory (and gave \
\"entangled states\" their name). 
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.457824440513422*^9, 3.457824486617043*^9}, {
   3.457824529161523*^9, 3.457824544870864*^9}, 3.457824675395093*^9, {
   3.4578253267763653`*^9, 3.457825347776301*^9}, {3.468621115953309*^9, 
   3.468621133487142*^9}},
 FontSize->18]
}, Open  ]]
