 CellChangeTimes->{{3.4567646889580727`*^9, 3.456764772081943*^9}, {
   3.456764853035265*^9, 3.456764899131316*^9}, {3.456764930119863*^9, 
   3.456764955006021*^9}, {3.456765045454646*^9, 3.456765094773337*^9}, {
   3.4567654397138577`*^9, 3.4567655977857513`*^9}, {3.4572143926313057`*^9, 
   3.4572144478679447`*^9}, {3.4572153556115294`*^9, 3.457215356173115*^9}, {
   3.4578254923091383`*^9, 3.4578255096641827`*^9}, 3.457826161459263*^9, 
   3.468518959282783*^9, {3.468622032004863*^9, 3.468622062497467*^9}}],

Cell[TextData[{
 "The Kochen-Sprecker theorem is the first of what has become a large \
population of \"",
 StyleBox["no-go theorems",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "\"\[LongDash]theorems that demonstrate in a variety of contexts that the \
conditions imposed on the \"hidden variable instruction sets\" pose ",
 StyleBox["combinatorial problems that possess no solution",
  FontWeight->"Bold",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
 ". \n   \nThe KS theorem excited physicists (tiny population of \
\"foundations physicists\") because it provides an instance of a ",
 StyleBox["non-statistical state-independent proof ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "of the impossibility of \"non-contextual\" hidden variable theories.\n   \n\
The KS theorem can be looked upon as a simplified derivation of an \
implication of Gleason' s theorem which, however, was unknown to Specker when \
he first took up work in this area (1960).\n\nThe KS theorem\[LongDash]the \
work of two \"applied logicians\"\[LongDash]involves an intricate argument \
concerning the orthogonality relations present in a certain collection of 117 \
rays in 3-space, and does not look much like \"physics\"!  Attempts were made \
to simplify the line of argument that KS had introduced\[LongDash]initially \
by F. J. Bellinfante in his ",
 StyleBox["A Survey of Hidden Variable Theories",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " (1973). \n\nSo far as is known, the simplest 3-dimensional version of the \
KS argument (Conway & Kochen: 2002) employs a collection of 31 rays. Much \
simpler\[LongDash]but weaker\[LongDash]versions (by David Mermin and others) \
live in Hilbert spaces of higher dimension. \n\nFor a sense of the density of \
publication in this area (particularly by the Chinese!) see"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.457826176009137*^9, {3.457826217525282*^9, 3.4578262382146873`*^9}, {
