Cell[TextData[{
 "J. Zimba & R. Penrose, \"On Bell non-locality without probabilities: more \
curious geometry,\" Stud. Hist. Phil. Sci. ",
 StyleBox["24",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", 697, (1993). See also J. E. Massad & P. K. Aravind, \"The Penrose \
dodecahedron revisited,\" AJP ",
 StyleBox["67",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", 631 (1993). ",
 StyleBox["It was a Tom Wieting seminar based upon the latter paper that \
first brought Penrose's work to my attention",
  FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]],
 ".\n\nPenrose's theorem exploits properties of 40 rays that live in a \
certain\n 4-dimensional Hilbert space.  20 of those\[LongDash]which Penrose \
calls \"ordinary rays,\" refer to the spin-one-half states that result when \
the spin of a spin-3/2 particle is measured along one or another of the 20 \
directions defined by the vertices of a dodecahedron. Here again, cubes play \
a central role in the discussion: the dodecahedral vertices are organized \
into subsets by the five cubes that can be inscribed within a dodecahedron, \
and by the two tetrahedra that can be inscribed within each such cube: "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.4578292254093847`*^9, 3.4578736910057364`*^9, {3.457926301286972*^9, 
