Cell[TextData[{
 "To his \"ordinary rays\" Penrose adjoints the 20 \"extraordinary rays\"\
\[LongDash]one associated with each vertex\[LongDash]defined by their \
orthogonality to the ordinary rays at the three nearest neighbors of that \
vertex.  From these ingredients, Penrose fashions what he calls a \
\"Quintessential Toy,\" in terms of which he poses an ",
 StyleBox["insoluable coloring problem",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". This he uses in Chapter 5 of ",
 StyleBox["Shadows of the Mind",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " (1994) to dramatize the fact that \"quantum mechanics in in some \
fundamental respects very strange, very counterintuitive.\" \n\nUltimately, \
Penrose places two such spin 3/2 particles in a singlet state, gives one to \
Alice, one to Bob, constructs an argument very like Bell's before him, and \
very like the argument adopted later by Conway & Kochen.\n\n"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.456775998137762*^9, 3.456776105605649*^9}, {
  3.456776285527732*^9, 3.456776343884602*^9}, {3.456776914657596*^9, 
  3.45677702431034*^9}, {3.456793291098172*^9, 3.4567933162044163`*^9}, {
  3.45679339403771*^9, 3.456793446640533*^9}, {3.4578294208709917`*^9, 
  3.4578295564718513`*^9}, {3.457926312964224*^9, 3.457926313138296*^9}, {
  3.468529998046736*^9, 3.4685300107045794`*^9}, {3.46862317875974*^9, 
  3.468623183684497*^9}, {3.468623229340941*^9, 3.468623231713455*^9}},
 FontSize->16],

