Cell[TextData[{
 "Physicists sometimes allow themselves to say \"the system ",
 StyleBox["wants",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to \[Ellipsis]\",  \"Nature might have been that way, or that other way, \
but ",
 StyleBox["chose",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " to be this way\[Ellipsis]\".  I try never to use, and always object to \
such anthropocentric language, but not so Conway (& Kochen), who carry it to \
a shameless extreme. It is my own suspicion that ",
 StyleBox["the laws of Nature cannot self-consistently be other than what \
they are, and that\[LongDash]contrary to the claims of some string theorists\
\[LongDash]no question of \"choice\" is involved",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ". Interestingly, Conway and other mathematicians never assert that revealed \
",
 StyleBox["mathematical",
  FontVariations->{"Underline"->True}],
 " facts \"might\" have been other than what they are found to be, and become \
nervous when mathematical arguments appeal to the Axiom of Choice.\n\n  "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.468630703727694*^9, 3.468630954328993*^9}, {
   3.468630984812377*^9, 3.468631284010867*^9}, {3.468631321784552*^9, 
   3.46863135804809*^9}, {3.468710476390106*^9, 3.468710536873712*^9}, 
   3.468713376226843*^9},
 FontSize->18],

Cell["\<\
Conway and Kochen can properly claim to have driven yet another nail into the \
hidden variables coffin, though some people find loopholes in their argument, \
to which they take technical exception:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.468693194569788*^9, 3.468693275532959*^9}, {
  3.468713014843843*^9, 3.4687130172602787`*^9}},
 FontSize->18],

